      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "Both ACE inhibitors (angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors) and ARBs (angiotensin II receptor blockers) are equally recommended as first-line therapies for treating high blood pressure in major medical guidelines (such as the ACC/AHA _azTuavbVDJabwbkPxYi0yAM_18 and ESC/ESH ).",
        "snippet_highlighted_words": [
          "Both ACE inhibitors (angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors) and ARBs (angiotensin II receptor blockers) are equally recommended as first-line therapies for treating high blood pressure"
        ],
        "reference_indexes": [
          0,
          1
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "How They Compare"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Efficacy: Both classes target the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system (RAAS) and lower blood pressure and protect the heart and kidneys with similar effectiveness .",
            "reference_indexes": [
              1,
              3,
              4
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Mechanism:",
            "list": [
              {
                "snippet": "ACE inhibitors (ending in -pril, e.g., lisinopril) block the enzyme that produces angiotensin II and also cause a buildup of bradykinin."
              },
              {
                "snippet": "ARBs (ending in -sartan, e.g., losartan) block angiotensin II directly at its receptor sites without affecting bradykinin levels.",
                "reference_indexes": [
                  4,
                  5,
                  6
                ]
              }
            ],
            "reference_indexes": [
              4,
              5,
              6
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Side Effects & Tolerability:",
            "list": [
              {
                "snippet": "ARBs generally have a better safety and tolerability profile ."
              },
              {
                "snippet": "ACE inhibitors frequently cause a persistent dry cough (in about 10% of patients) and carry a risk of angioedema (swelling of the face or tongue). These side effects are rare with ARBs."
              },
              {
                "snippet": "Both classes can increase blood potassium levels (hyperkalemia) and require monitoring of kidney function .",
                "reference_indexes": [
                  0,
                  2,
                  6,
                  7,
                  8,
                  9
                ]
              }
            ],
            "reference_indexes": [
              0,
              2,
              6,
              7,
              8,
              9
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Clinical Preference: While ACE inhibitors have historically been prescribed more often because they have been on the market longer, large-scale studies (like the ) support preferentially starting with an ARB due to lower discontinuation rates and fewer side effects.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              0,
              5
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "For comprehensive details on how these medications compare and function, you can review the National Kidney Foundation Overview _azTuavbVDJabwbkPxYi0yAM_22 or the American Heart Association Research Study _azTuavbVDJabwbkPxYi0yAM_23 .",
        "reference_indexes": [
          0,
          7
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "To help me narrow this down for your situation, let me know:"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Are you starting treatment for the first time or looking to switch from an existing medication?"
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Do you have any other conditions like kidney disease, diabetes, or a history of cough/angioedema?"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
